speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, the Government of the Prime Minister (Mr. Mulroney) has knuckled under pressure from the American drug lobby. Bill C-22 is another pawn, another attempt to get a free trade ride with a lame duck President. Senior citizens, the sick and the disabled will bear the brunt of this give-away.
On March 10, 1987, the P.E.I. Legislature unanimously passed the following resolution:
WHEREAS the Government of Canada has introduced amendments to the Patent Act which will inexorably over time result in an increase in the price of prescription drugs;
AND WHEREAS the Province of Prince Edward Island has recently introduced a Drug Cost Assistance Plan which strives to reduce the cost-burden of drugs for senior citizens;
AND WHEREAS increased prescription costs will adversely affect this Plan and indeed all other citizens of our Province who require drugs from time to time;
THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED that this Assembly urge the Prime Minister in the strongest possible terms not to proceed with this Bill.

Mr. Speaker, the formula mentioned in Part VI of the Meech Lake Accord where it is provided that annual conferences of the First Ministers shall be convened once a year, commencing in 1988. Section 2 reads as follows:
(2) The conferences convened under subsection (1) shall have included on their agenda the following matters:
(a) Senate reform, including the role and functions of the Senate, its powers, the method of selecting Senators and representation in the Senate;

Mr. Speaker, it is again my duty today to table a petition signed by residents of Canada who now avail themselves of their ancient and undoubted right thus to present a grievance common to your petitioners in the certain assurance that our Honourable House will therefore provide a remedy. The petition reads as follows:
Whereas since September, 1984, the federal Government has imposed upon Canadian taxpayers 25 new taxes or tax increases, in particular on medical and dental supplies, snack food, candies and soft drinks;
Whereas the federal Government in its tax reform proposal is studying the possibility of imposing a general tax on goods and services, including food;
And whereas food constitutes a vital element to every-day life, and whereas the imposition of this tax would have the effect of penalizing certain categories of taxpayers, for example, the aged, families, the unemployed, low income taxpayers, and youth;
Therefore, the petitioners humbly pray and call upon Parliament to reject all measures that would have the effect of imposing a sales tax on food products.
